"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[30502],{30502:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"content":"\\n## ComfyUI Model Guide - Checkpoint, LoRA, VAE, and How to Choose the Right Model\\n```require\\n- ComfyUI: Based on the latest stable version\\n- Model info: As of mid-2026 (models change rapidly)\\n```\\n\\n### Introduction\\nWhen you launch ComfyUI for the first time, connecting nodes is usually manageable. The real confusion starts right after. Inside the `models` directory, you find a long list of subfolders like checkpoint, LoRA, VAE, clip, and controlnet. On Civitai or Hugging Face, thousands of models are listed, and their names alone rarely make their differences clear. Terms like \\"Base,\\" \\"Merge,\\" \\"Turbo,\\" \\"Pony,\\" \\"Illustrious,\\" and \\"Flux\\" appear everywhere, yet few places explain what they actually mean.\\n\\nThis article does not cover how models are trained. Instead, it explains from a user\'s practical perspective <mark>what role each file serves, how to choose the right one for your scenario, where to place it, and how to use it</mark>.\\n\\n| Question | What This Guide Covers |\\n|---|---|\\n| What is each file in the models folder? | Folder roles and node connections |\\n| What is the difference between Checkpoint, Base, and Merge? | Distinguishing a model\'s \\"origin\\" |\\n| Which should I use: SD1.5, SDXL, Pony, or Flux? | Family characteristics and practical use cases |\\n| How do I use LoRAs? | Strength, trigger words, and compatibility |\\n| What if errors occur or results look distorted? | Compatibility rules and causes by symptom |\\n\\n\\n```adcard\\n```\\n\\n### The Big Picture: What Goes Into One Image\\nA single generated image is the result of multiple model files working in sequence. Understanding which stage of the pipeline each file handles clarifies almost every folder name.\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart TB\\n    A[\\"Text Encoder<br/>(CLIP / T5)\\"] --> B[\\"Diffusion Model<br/>(UNet / DiT)\\"]\\n    L[\\"LoRA\\"] -.-> B\\n    L -.-> A\\n    C[\\"ControlNet\\"] -.-> B\\n    B --> D[\\"VAE Decode\\"]\\n    D --> E[\\"Upscale Model\\"]\\n```\\n\\n| Stage | What It Does | File Type |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Prompt Interpretation | Translates text into numbers the model understands | Text Encoder (CLIP, T5) |\\n| Image Generation | Progressively shapes an image out of noise | Diffusion Model (UNet, DiT) |\\n| Adjustment | Modifies art style, character, or composition | LoRA, ControlNet, Embedding |\\n| Pixel Conversion | Decodes the internal latent representation into real pixels | VAE |\\n| Post-processing | Upscales resolution and refines details | Upscale Model, Detailer |\\n\\nThe core rule is that <mark>the Diffusion Model is the center, and everything else must match that model</mark>. This rule will come up repeatedly throughout this guide.\\n\\n### The models Folder Map\\nIn a default installation, models reside under `ComfyUI/models/`. After placing your files there, refresh the browser (`R` key) or restart ComfyUI, and they will appear in the node dropdown menus.\\n\\n| Folder | What Goes In | Nodes Used |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `checkpoints` | All-in-one models (Diffusion + CLIP + VAE) | Load Checkpoint |\\n| `diffusion_models` (formerly `unet`) | Standalone Diffusion Models, GGUF files | Load Diffusion Model, UNet Loader |\\n| `text_encoders` (formerly `clip`) | Text parsing models such as CLIP and T5 | Load CLIP, DualCLIP Loader |\\n| `vae` | Standalone VAE files | Load VAE |\\n| `loras` | LoRA files | Load LoRA |\\n| `controlnet` | ControlNet, T2I-Adapter | Load ControlNet Model |\\n| `embeddings` | Textual Inversion | `embedding:name` in prompts |\\n| `upscale_models` | ESRGAN-family upscalers | Load Upscale Model |\\n| `clip_vision` | Image-understanding CLIP models (for IP-Adapter, Redux) | Load CLIP Vision |\\n| `ipadapter` | IP-Adapter models (requires custom node) | IPAdapter nodes |\\n| `ultralytics/bbox`, `sams` | Face detection, segmentation (for Impact Pack) | Detector, SAM Loader |\\n\\n> If you already use Automatic1111 or another UI, you do not need to duplicate your model files. Rename `extra_model_paths.yaml.example` in your ComfyUI root folder to `extra_model_paths.yaml`, specify your paths inside, and both tools can share the same directories. You don\'t have to keep duplicate copies of multi-gigabyte models.\\n\\n### Checkpoint: The All-in-One File\\nA Checkpoint is the most common file type you will download. Because it bundles the Diffusion Model, Text Encoder, and VAE into a single file, <mark>a single Load Checkpoint node outputs all three: MODEL, CLIP, and VAE</mark>. Initially, connecting just this node to a KSampler and VAE Decode is enough to produce an image.\\n\\n| Load Checkpoint Output | Where to Connect |\\n|---|---|\\n| MODEL | KSampler (or through a LoRA if using one) |\\n| CLIP | CLIP Text Encode (prompt input node) |\\n| VAE | VAE Decode |\\n\\n#### Checkpoint vs split models\\nSD1.5 and SDXL are commonly distributed as all-in-one Checkpoints. In contrast, modern models such as Flux, SD3.5, Qwen-Image, and Wan are frequently <mark>distributed in modular components</mark>. Because these models are so large, the Text Encoder alone can take up several gigabytes.\\n\\n| Category | All-in-One (Checkpoint) | Modular (Split) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Files | 1 file | Diffusion Model + Text Encoder + VAE |\\n| Folder | `checkpoints` | `diffusion_models`, `text_encoders`, `vae` |\\n| Nodes | Load Checkpoint | Load Diffusion Model + Load CLIP + Load VAE |\\n| Advantages | Simple to set up | Allows swapping Text Encoders to different precisions or sharing across models |\\n| Examples | SD1.5, SDXL families | Flux, SD3.5, Z-Image, Qwen-Image, Wan |\\n\\nFor modular models, loading an official workflow (example JSON) first is the easiest route. The node wiring and expected filenames are already configured for you. Model-specific templates can be found under `Workflow → Browse Templates` inside ComfyUI.\\n\\n### File Formats and Precision\\nEven for the exact same model, suffixes appended to the filename indicate significant differences in size and security.\\n\\n#### Extension\\n| Extension | Description | Recommendation |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `.safetensors` | Safe format containing raw numbers (tensors) only | Always download this |\\n| `.ckpt` | Based on Python pickle; can execute arbitrary code when loaded | Avoid whenever possible |\\n| `.gguf` | Format for quantized large models (requires `ComfyUI-GGUF` custom node) | When VRAM is limited |\\n| `.pt`, `.pth`, `.bin` | Legacy formats; still found in some upscalers | Only from trusted sources |\\n\\n#### Precision (fp32, fp16, bf16, fp8)\\nPrecision refers to how many bits are used to store each numeric value. Fewer bits result in smaller file sizes and lower VRAM usage, though a subtle loss in quality may occur.\\n\\n| Notation | Meaning | Characteristics |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| fp32 | 32-bit | Largest file size; rarely used |\\n| fp16, bf16 | 16-bit | Standard default; practically zero quality loss |\\n| fp8 (`e4m3fn`, `e5m2`) | 8-bit | Half the size; differences are often imperceptible to the eye |\\n| Q8, Q5, Q4 (GGUF) | Quantized | Smaller numbers use less memory with slight, progressive quality degradation |\\n\\nYou will also frequently encounter labels like `pruned` and `ema` in checkpoint names. A `pruned` file strips out training-only weights; users generating images should download this version. As a general rule of thumb, an SD1.5 fp16 model is around 2GB, while an SDXL fp16 model is around 6–7GB.\\n\\n### Base, Fine-tune, Merge: Where a Model Comes From\\nCheckpoints on Civitai fall broadly into three categories based on \\"how they were created.\\" Knowing this distinction makes reading model descriptions much easier.\\n\\n| Type | How It Was Made | Characteristics | Examples |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Base (Original) | Trained from scratch by an organization on large datasets | General-purpose; lacks specific style biases, acting as the foundation for other models | SD1.5, SDXL 1.0, Flux.1 dev |\\n| Fine-tune | Base model trained on additional curated data | Specialized toward a specific domain (anime, realism, specific subjects) | Pony Diffusion, Illustrious XL |\\n| Merge | Weighted combination of multiple models | Blended without training to achieve appealing, customized aesthetics | Most popular models on Civitai |\\n\\n#### Are Merges Bad?\\nNot at all. You simply need to understand their trade-offs.\\n\\n- <b>Pros:</b> They combine the strengths of multiple models, producing appealing results right out of the box.\\n- <b>Cons:</b> It is often unclear which models were blended in what ratios, and prompt adherence varies between checkpoints. Licenses also become mixed.\\n- <b>Tip:</b> For beginners, highly rated merges are often the easiest to use. Advanced users often prefer taking a Base or Fine-tune model and layering LoRAs for precise control.\\n\\n\\n```adcard\\n```\\n\\n#### Distilled: Turbo, Lightning, Hyper, schnell\\nThese are models distilled to generate images in significantly fewer steps. They reduce generations that originally required 20–30 steps down to 1–8 steps.\\n\\n| Name | Typical Steps | CFG | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| SDXL Turbo, SD Turbo | 1–4 | Around 1 | Focused on speed; lower base resolution |\\n| SDXL-Lightning, Hyper-SD | 2–8 | 1–2 | Also distributed as LoRAs that can be applied to any SDXL model |\\n| LCM | 4–8 | 1–2 | LoRA-based acceleration; more sensitive to settings |\\n| Flux.1 schnell | 1–4 | 1 | Permissive license (Apache 2.0) |\\n\\nWhile excellent for rapid composition sketching, standard models usually offer better fine detail and prompt fidelity. A common workflow is sketching compositions with a Turbo model, then switching to a standard model for the final render.\\n\\n#### Other labels you will see\\n| Label | Meaning |\\n|---|---|\\n| Inpainting | Models specialized for repainting selected image regions. Many standard models handle inpainting well today, making dedicated models optional |\\n| Refiner | Auxiliary models designed to polish late-stage SDXL steps. Rarely used today |\\n| Edit / Kontext | Instruction-based editing models where you provide an image and instruct \\"modify it like this\\" |\\n| NSFW / SFW | The bias of the training data. Check the model page guidelines and licensing |\\n\\n### Model Families: Which One Should I Use\\nRegardless of how many model names exist, they all trace back to \\"which Base they originated from.\\" <mark>Only models within the exact same lineage can share LoRAs, ControlNets, and VAEs</mark>.\\n\\n| Family | Base Resolution | Prompt Style | VRAM Baseline | Strengths |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| SD 1.5 | 512 | Short tags + sentences | 4–6GB | Lightweight with the largest ecosystem; vast library of legacy LoRAs and ControlNets |\\n| SDXL | 1024 | Tags + sentences | 8GB+ | Balanced, general-purpose; has the widest variety of choices |\\n| Pony / Illustrious / NoobAI | 1024 | Tag-focused | 8GB+ | Anime, illustration, character understanding (SDXL-based) |\\n| Flux.1 (dev, schnell) | 1024+ | Natural language | 12GB+ (reducible via fp8, GGUF) | Realistic rendering, hands, text rendering, long prompt comprehension |\\n| SD 3.5 (Large, Medium) | 1024 | Natural language | 8GB+ | High prompt fidelity; check terms for licensing |\\n| Z-Image (Turbo) | 1024+ | Natural language | 12–16GB | Fast realistic photos with a compact model (~8 steps); renders Korean, Chinese, and English text |\\n| Qwen-Image | 1024+ | Natural language | High | Exceptional text rendering inside images (including Korean) |\\n| Flux.1 Kontext, Qwen-Image-Edit | Based on input image | Instructions | High | Edits existing images based on instructions |\\n| Wan, HunyuanVideo, LTX-Video | Video | Natural language | High | Text/Image-to-Video generation |\\n\\n#### Choosing by Use Case\\n| What You Want to Do | Recommended Choice |\\n|---|---|\\n| First time; just want to generate something working | A popular SDXL-based merge |\\n| Anime, illustration, specific characters | Illustrious / NoobAI family (or Pony) |\\n| Photorealistic people or products | Flux.1 dev family or an SDXL realism fine-tune |\\n| Rendering text inside images | Flux, Qwen-Image, or Z-Image |\\n| Fast photo-style images without an ultra-high-end GPU | Z-Image Turbo (~8 steps) |\\n| VRAM is 8GB or less | SD1.5, SDXL (fp16), or Flux GGUF Q4–Q5 |\\n| Editing existing photos with instructions | Flux.1 Kontext, Qwen-Image-Edit |\\n| Short video clips | Wan family |\\n| Commercial use | Check the model card license first (Flux dev has non-commercial terms) |\\n\\n#### Practical Tips by Family\\n- <b>SD1.5:</b> Being an older architecture, keep resolution around 512px and upscale afterward for high-res outputs. Anime checkpoints frequently require Clip skip 2.\\n- <b>SDXL:</b> Generate around 1024x1024 (or equivalent surface areas like 1216x832, 832x1216). Straying too far from native resolution causes composition issues.\\n- <b>Pony:</b> Requires prepending quality tags (such as `score_9, score_8_up, score_7_up`).\\n- <b>Illustrious / NoobAI:</b> Primarily driven by Danbooru tags. Follow the quality tags recommended on the model card.\\n- <b>Z-Image Turbo:</b> Being modular, you download the Diffusion Model, Text Encoder (Qwen-series LLM), and VAE separately. Set steps to ~8, CFG around 1, and prompt using descriptive sentences. Using the official template workflow is easiest.\\n- <b>Flux:</b> Prompt using natural language sentences. Keep CFG at 1 and adjust guidance scale (~3.5) with the FluxGuidance node. Note that negative prompts are practically ignored by Flux dev.\\n\\nBecause recommended parameters vary by model, <mark>following the Sampler, Steps, CFG, and Clip skip recommendations on the model details page</mark> is always the fastest path to good results.\\n\\n| Family | Steps | CFG | Example Samplers |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Z-Image Turbo | ~8 | 1 | Follow model card |\\n| SD1.5 / SDXL | 20–30 | 5–8 | `dpmpp_2m` + `karras`, `euler_ancestral` |\\n| Illustrious / Pony | 25–30 | 4–7 | `euler_ancestral`, `dpmpp_2m` |\\n| Flux dev | 20–30 | 1 (+ FluxGuidance 3.5) | `euler` + `simple` |\\n| Flux schnell | 1–4 | 1 | `euler` + `simple` |\\n| Turbo / Lightning | 2–8 | 1–2 | Follow model card |\\n\\n### LoRA: Small Add-on Files\\nA LoRA is a compact modifier file applied on top of a Checkpoint. Weighing only tens to hundreds of megabytes, it lets you introduce specific characters, artistic styles, clothing, or poses without having to download an entirely new base model.\\n\\n| LoRA Type | Purpose | Example |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Character | Specific individuals or characters | Preserving facial features and outfits |\\n| Style | Art styles and palettes | Watercolor, specific artist styles |\\n| Concept | Specific objects, props, poses | Specialized clothing, backgrounds |\\n| Detail / Slider | Single-axis adjustments like detail, age, or expression | Slider LoRAs |\\n| Speed | Enables generation with fewer steps | Lightning, Hyper, LCM |\\n\\n#### How to use it in ComfyUI\\n1. Place the file in `models/loras` and refresh.\\n2. Wire the Load LoRA node downstream of the Load Checkpoint node. It accepts MODEL and CLIP inputs and outputs modified MODEL and CLIP signals.\\n3. Pass the resulting MODEL to KSampler, and CLIP to CLIP Text Encode.\\n4. To apply multiple LoRAs, chain Load LoRA nodes sequentially.\\n\\n| Node Input | Meaning | Starting Value |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| strength_model | Influence on image generation model | 0.6 – 1.0 |\\n| strength_clip | Influence on text interpretation | Same as strength_model, or lower if issues occur |\\n\\nFor architectures that do not modify the Text Encoder, such as Flux or video models, use the `LoraLoaderModelOnly` node instead.\\n\\n#### Essential Rules When Using LoRAs\\n- <b>Lineages must match.</b> An SD1.5 LoRA cannot be used on SDXL. A Flux LoRA only works on Flux.\\n- <b>Check trigger words.</b> Many LoRAs require specific trigger keywords in the prompt to activate. These are listed on the model page.\\n- <b>Start with lower strength.</b> If faces look distorted or colors appear oversaturated/burned at 1.0, lower it to 0.6–0.8.\\n- <b>Stacking too many causes interference.</b> If total cumulative strength exceeds 1.5–2.0, the image is prone to breaking down.\\n- <b>Even within SDXL, using a Pony LoRA on Illustrious (or vice versa)</b> often results in poor compatibility. Always check which base architecture the LoRA was trained on.\\n\\n### VAE, Text Encoder, Embedding\\n#### VAE\\nThe VAE converts compressed latent representations from the model into actual pixel images. Most Checkpoints bundle a built-in VAE, so downloading a separate one is usually unnecessary. Connect an external VAE only when troubleshooting specific visual flaws.\\n\\n| Symptom | Cause & Solution |\\n|---|---|\\n| Colors look dull and washed out | The embedded VAE is weak. Connect the model\'s recommended VAE using Load VAE |\\n| Pure black images on SDXL | fp16 NaN overflow issue. Use a patched VAE such as `sdxl-vae-fp16-fix` |\\n| Strange colors on Flux/SD3 | Flux requires its dedicated `ae.safetensors`. It is incompatible with SD1.5 or SDXL VAEs |\\n\\n\\n```adcard\\n```\\n\\n#### Text Encoder (CLIP, T5)\\nTranslates prompt text into numerical vectors that the model understands. Different families use different combinations.\\n\\n| Family | Text Encoder |\\n|---|---|\\n| SD1.5 | CLIP-L |\\n| SDXL | CLIP-L + CLIP-G |\\n| Flux, SD3.5 | CLIP-L + T5-XXL (for long sentence comprehension) |\\n| Z-Image, Qwen-Image | Qwen-family LLMs (for multilingual comprehension, including Korean) |\\n\\nT5-XXL is large—around 9–10GB in fp16—so fp8 variants are commonly used when memory is tight. For SD1.5 anime models, setting a `CLIP Set Last Layer` node to -2 is equivalent to Clip skip 2.\\n\\n#### Embedding (Textual Inversion)\\nVery small files (tens of KB) that package a specific concept into a single keyword. Place them in `models/embeddings` and invoke them in your prompt using `embedding:filename`. Negative embeddings like `EasyNegative` were once common, but their usage has declined with modern models and LoRAs.\\n\\n### ControlNet, IP-Adapter and Upscalers\\n#### ControlNet: Control the Composition\\nControlNet extracts structural guides—such as outlines, depth maps, or poses—from a reference image to lock down the composition. Preprocessor nodes are available via the `comfyui_controlnet_aux` custom node pack.\\n\\n| Type | Input Feature Used | Best Used For |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Canny | Edge outlines | Preserving precise silhouettes while changing art style |\\n| Depth | Depth / distance data | Maintaining spatial depth and perspective |\\n| OpenPose | Human skeletal keypoints | Explicitly directing human poses |\\n| Lineart / Scribble | Line drawings, rough sketches | Turning concept sketches into finished art |\\n| Tile | Original image textures | Preserving textures while upscaling and adding detail |\\n\\nControlNet models <mark>must also match the base family</mark>. SD1.5 ControlNets cannot be used with SDXL, and Flux uses dedicated ControlNet or Union models.\\n\\n#### IP-Adapter and Redux: Image as a Prompt\\nTransfers styles or subject likeness using reference images instead of text prompts. IP-Adapter requires a custom node (`ComfyUI_IPAdapter_plus`) and a `clip_vision` model; Redux serves an equivalent role in the Flux ecosystem. It is an excellent alternative when training a custom LoRA is impractical.\\n\\n#### Upscale Models\\nThese are ESRGAN-based models used via `Load Upscale Model` → `Upscale Image (using Model)`. They scale resolution 2x to 4x instantly without additional training. Pick a model tailored to your style: anime upscalers for anime art, and photo upscalers for photography.\\n\\n| Method | Description | Characteristics |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Upscale Model | Directly scales the pixel image | Fast and highly consistent |\\n| Latent Upscale + Resampling | Enlarges the latent and regenerates | Introduces new fine details, but high denoise values can alter shapes |\\n| Hybrid Approach | Scale with model first, then refine with low denoise (0.2–0.4) | The most widely used approach |\\n\\n#### Detailer (Impact Pack)\\nWhen faces render small in the frame and lose detail, this technique detects the face region and repaints just that segment at higher resolution. Place detection models in `ultralytics/bbox` and segmentation models in `sams`. Installing Impact Pack via ComfyUI Manager downloads the required components.\\n\\n### Compatibility Rules: Mix Only What Matches\\nWhenever an error occurs or the image looks broken, mismatched model families are almost always the cause.\\n\\n| Combination | Works? | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| SDXL Checkpoint + SDXL LoRA | Yes | Ideal pairing |\\n| SDXL Checkpoint + SD1.5 LoRA | No | Produces errors or gibberish output |\\n| Pony Checkpoint + Illustrious LoRA | Mostly awkward | Both originate from SDXL, but different tuning approaches cause poor compatibility |\\n| SD1.5 Checkpoint + SDXL ControlNet | No | Architectures must match |\\n| Flux + SDXL VAE | No | Flux requires its dedicated VAE |\\n| SDXL Checkpoint + Different SDXL VAE | Yes | Useful to fix color or NaN issues |\\n\\nError logs often tell the story. Messages like `mat1 and mat2 shapes cannot be multiplied` or `size mismatch` almost always indicate that <mark>models from different families were wired together</mark>.\\n\\n### VRAM Guide\\nBecause ComfyUI aggressively conserves memory by loading and unloading models on demand, workflows can often complete even when memory is tight, albeit more slowly. The following can serve as a baseline when planning your setup.\\n\\n| VRAM | Comfortable Usage | Fallbacks When Memory Is Tight |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 6–8GB | SD1.5, SDXL (fp16) | Lower resolution; use tiled VAE |\\n| 12GB | General SDXL, Flux fp8, Z-Image Turbo | Flux GGUF Q5 |\\n| 16GB | Flux dev fp8, SD3.5 Large | Shorter frame counts for video models |\\n| 24GB+ | Flux fp16, Qwen-Image, Video models | |\\n\\nWhen memory runs short, try: ① fp8 or GGUF variants of the model, ② an fp8 version of heavy Text Encoders like T5, and ③ the `--lowvram` launch flag. If an Out Of Memory (OOM) error occurs, lowering the generation resolution is the fastest fix.\\n\\n### Where to Get Models and How to Read a Model Page\\n| Source | Characteristics |\\n|---|---|\\n| Hugging Face | Official releases. Base models, split components, and official workflow templates reside here |\\n| Civitai | Community models. Largest collection of Checkpoints and LoRAs, with image galleries to preview styles |\\n| ComfyUI Manager | Search and install models and custom nodes directly from within ComfyUI |\\n\\nKey fields to check on a Civitai model page:\\n\\n| Field | What to Check |\\n|---|---|\\n| Base Model | Architecture family (SD 1.5, SDXL 1.0, Pony, Illustrious, Flux.1 D, etc.). Determines compatibility with your LoRAs |\\n| Type | Checkpoint, LoRA, VAE, Embedding, ControlNet |\\n| Version Notes | Recommended Sampler, Steps, CFG, Clip skip, and VAE settings |\\n| Trigger Words | Specific keywords required to activate the LoRA |\\n| License | Commercial use permissions and restrictions |\\n| Preview Images | Click the info button on sample images to view exact prompts and generation workflows |\\n\\nPre-download checklist:\\n\\n- Confirm the file extension is <b>`.safetensors`</b>.\\n- Verify that the <b>Base Model</b> matches your target workflow family.\\n- Select the precision (<b>fp16, fp8, pruned</b>) that matches your VRAM capacity.\\n- Check the <b>license</b> and source. Avoid unfamiliar `.ckpt` files or archives bundled with `.exe` files.\\n\\n### Troubleshooting\\n| Symptom | Possible Cause | Solution |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Model does not appear in dropdown | Wrong folder location or interface not refreshed | Move to correct folder and press `R` |\\n| `mat1 and mat2 shapes...` error | Incompatible model families connected | Match the Base Model across Checkpoint, LoRA, and ControlNet |\\n| Solid black image | fp16 VAE NaN overflow, incorrect VAE | Connect a patched VAE or an fp32 VAE |\\n| Washed out / dull colors | VAE issue | Use the recommended VAE |\\n| LoRA produces no noticeable effect | Missing trigger word, strength too low, mismatched family | Add trigger word, increase strength, verify family match |\\n| Distorted, messy generation | Resolution deviates significantly from native base | Keep SDXL around 1024px |\\n| Malformed human anatomy | CFG set too high, LoRA over-application | Lower CFG and reduce LoRA strength |\\n| Negative prompt has no effect on Flux | Flux dev is designed without negative prompting | Describe desired attributes affirmatively in the main prompt |\\n| Out Of Memory | Insufficient VRAM | Reduce resolution, use fp8/GGUF, pass `--lowvram` |\\n| Image finishes too quickly and looks corrupted | Standard settings (CFG 7, 30 steps) applied to Turbo/Lightning | Adjust to 4–8 steps and CFG 1–2 |\\n\\n\\n```adcard\\n```\\n\\n### Summary\\nHere is a recap of what we covered in this post:\\n\\n| Topic | Key Takeaway |\\n|---|---|\\n| models Folder | Each file type has a dedicated folder and node; refresh the UI to update dropdowns |\\n| Checkpoint | All-in-one bundle containing Diffusion + CLIP + VAE; newer architectures lean modular |\\n| File Formats | Always choose `.safetensors`, and select fp16 / fp8 / GGUF to fit your available VRAM |\\n| Base / Fine-tune / Merge | Original, specialized, or blended weights. Popular merges offer the smoothest start for beginners |\\n| Distilled | Turbo, Lightning, and schnell achieve fast results with few steps, but require lower CFG settings |\\n| Model Families | Choose among SD1.5, SDXL, Pony / Illustrious, Flux, Z-Image, Qwen-Image, and video models based on use case and VRAM |\\n| LoRA | Match the base family, check trigger words, and start with conservative strength values |\\n| Auxiliary Models | VAE, Text Encoder, ControlNet, IP-Adapter, Upscalers, and Detailers |\\n| Compatibility Rules | Components only blend within the same family; errors almost always stem from architecture mismatches |"}')}}]);